<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

    <Import Project="..\CommonSettings.props" />

    <PropertyGroup>
        <Title>Stride Community Toolkit</Title>
        <Description>Helpers and extensions for the Stride Game Engine, for code-only projects and Game Studio projects alike. Thin wrappers over Stride's own APIs: they shorten the common tasks without hiding the engine.

What's inside:
- Code-only bootstrap: game.Run(start, update), SetupBase3D / SetupBase2D, default graphics compositor, camera, light and ground in one call
- Entity, Game, Camera, Transform, Model and Script System extensions: component search, LookAt, screen-to-world rays, delays, repeats and timers
- Procedural primitives (cube, sphere, capsule, cylinder, cone, torus, teapot, plane and 2D shapes) with materials, no assets required
- MeshBuilder and TextureCanvas for building meshes and textures in code
- On-screen text: TextOverlay (scalable, high-DPI aware, per-line background), EntityTextComponent and WorldTextComponent
- Camera controllers for 3D (fly) and 2D (pan and zoom) scenes with built-in key help
- Easing functions, math and random helpers, collection helpers
- WindowsDpiManager: opt the process into per-monitor DPI awareness from code, no app.manifest needed, and check that the declaration took (Windows only; a no-op elsewhere)
- Brings in Stride.AssetCompiler as a build-only dependency: a code-only project has no assets, but the engine's shaders and default font still have to be compiled into its data folder, and this reference is what makes that happen

Physics helpers are in Stride.CommunityToolkit.Bepu (recommended) and Stride.CommunityToolkit.Bullet. Vector shapes with pixel-constant outlines are in Stride.CommunityToolkit.Shapes, and immediate-mode 3D debug primitives in Stride.CommunityToolkit.DebugShapes. On Linux, add Stride.CommunityToolkit.Linux for the asset compiler's native libraries.$(StrideVersionNote)
        </Description>
        <AllowUnsafeBlocks>true</AllowUnsafeBlocks>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(Configuration)|$(Platform)'=='Debug|AnyCPU'">
        <DefineConstants>$(DefineConstants);CODE_ANALYSIS</DefineConstants>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <Using Include="Stride.Core" />
        <Using Include="Stride.Core.Mathematics" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <!-- Open Sans Regular (Apache 2.0, licence beside it): the font MeshFont.Default opens, so solid lettering
             works with no font file at hand and gives the same shapes on every machine. -->
        <EmbeddedResource Include="Rendering\Text\Fonts\OpenSans-Regular.ttf" LogicalName="Stride.CommunityToolkit.Rendering.Text.Fonts.OpenSans-Regular.ttf" />
        <None Include="Rendering\Text\Fonts\OpenSans-LICENSE.txt" Pack="true" PackagePath="fonts/OpenSans-LICENSE.txt" />
        <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Stride.CommunityToolkit.Tests" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <PackageReference Include="Stride.Engine" Version="$(StrideVersion)" />
        <PackageReference Include="Stride.Particles" Version="$(StrideVersion)" />
        <PackageReference Include="Stride.UI" Version="$(StrideVersion)" />
        <!-- The build step that compiles the engine's shaders and default font into a game's data folder. A
             code-only game has no assets of its own and still needs it, and a Game Studio game already carries the
             same reference, which NuGet merges into one. Build-only: the package's own assemblies must not reach a
             game (its lib/ assembly fails at start-up on Stride.NuGetResolver), hence IncludeAssets. The compile
             step itself runs only for executables (StrideIsExecutable in the package's targets), so libraries,
             tests and the docs build are untouched. -->
        <PackageReference Include="Stride.AssetCompiler" Version="$(StrideVersion)" IncludeAssets="build;buildTransitive" />
        <!-- Stride's desktop window is SDL and ships this binding, but only as a runtime dependency;
             DisplayScale asks SDL for the display DPI, which needs it at compile time. Keep the
             version at what Stride's own Stride.Graphics references, or NuGet reports a downgrade. -->
        <PackageReference Include="Silk.NET.SDL" Version="2.23.0" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <Compile Update="Engine\EntityExtensions.GetComponents.cs">
            <DesignTime>True</DesignTime>
            <AutoGen>True</AutoGen>
            <DependentUpon>EntityExtensions.GetComponents.tt</DependentUpon>
        </Compile>
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <None Update="Engine\EntityExtensions.GetComponents.tt">
            <Generator>TextTemplatingFileGenerator</Generator>
            <LastGenOutput>EntityExtensions.GetComponents.cs</LastGenOutput>
        </None>
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <Service Include="{508349b6-6b84-4df5-91f0-309beebad82d}" />
    </ItemGroup>

</Project>